Third-party components in or used by HakiOS
(full table with origins and status: THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md; compliance notes: COMPLIANCE.md)

In the repository:

- XNU (Apple): the kernel sources are fetched by xnu/apply.sh from github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu;
  our patch and files are a derivative work under the Apple Public Source License 2.0 (LICENSE.APSL).
- libdeflate (Eric Biggers), xnu/files/bsd/miscfs/apfs/libdeflate/: MIT, see its COPYING.
- mkbootimg.py (Android Open Source Project), devices/redwood/prebuilt/mkbootimg.py: Apache License 2.0.
- Mu-Silicium / Project Mu (Project Silicium, Microsoft and contributors): devices/redwood/uefi/ holds a
  patch and files against it; their license applies to the derived parts (BSD-2-Clause-Patent).
- UEFI driver binaries of the phone's own firmware, devices/redwood/uefi/files/Binaries/redwood/: the
  vendor's (Qualcomm/Xiaomi), extracted from the phone's xbl.img the way Project Silicium redistributes
  such drivers for other phones. They are needed only to rebuild the UEFI image; they can be removed and
  extracted by each user from their own phone's firmware with UEFIReader.

- the FocalTech touch firmware, drivers/input/hakios.focaltech_fw.h: the touch vendor's;
  generated from a device blob, redistribution terms "to verify" (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md).

- the Wi-Fi driver: Linux's ath11k (BSD-3-Clause-Clear) and FreeBSD's LinuxKPI headers (BSD-2-Clause) are
  fetched by tools/mk-ath11k.sh at pinned releases and built on our layer wifi/lx (MIT); only the layer is here.

- patches to Mesa, tools/mesa/*.patch, and the libdrm stand-in tools/mesa/hakios-drm/: Mesa's and X.Org's MIT terms.
- patches to UniXNU's AdrenoMetal, tools/m2v/adrenometal-ios16.patch: MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 NativeMetal contributors.
- a patch to steelbrain's metal2vulkan, tools/m2v/metal2vulkan-hakios.patch: GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, as the code it
  changes, not MIT.
- userland/src/HakiosMetalCA.m: begun from vcvkk's plug-in (github.com/vcvkk/darwin-redwood, branch hakios-workspace, at eb9fdf5),
  with this tree's changes. That origin's license is "to verify" (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md).

Fetched at build time, never committed:

- the Darwin userland 16.5 IPSW (Apple): the user space of the system image; the user gets it and
  sysimage/unpack.sh unpacks it on their machine; nothing downloads it; its license is Apple's.
- the iPhoneOS 16.5 SDK from github.com/theos/sdks, and theos's cctools (Apple's open-source cctools): the
  kernel's and user space's build tools.
- the phone's firmware (GPU, Wi-Fi, audio DSP, modem, haptics, cameras): the vendor's, pulled from the
  user's own phone (devices/redwood/pull-firmware.sh).
- lzfse (Apple, BSD-3-Clause), built by xnuloader/mkidentity.sh when no LZFSE decoder is installed.
- Mesa (for GL=1 images; Turnip for TURNIP=1, tools/mk-turnip.sh), Procursus and Sileo packages (tools/mk-jb.sh): their own licenses.
- hakios-metal (oosexgang, MIT), taken from beside this tree by userland/build.sh METAL_PLUGIN=ca.
- for M2V=1, by tools/mk-m2v.sh, at pinned revisions: UniXNU's AdrenoMetal 243d63c (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 NativeMetal contributors);
  steelbrain's metal2vulkan 624bab3 (GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later) with AdrenoMetal's 30 patches; the Rust crates of its
  Cargo.lock (MIT, Apache-2.0, ISC, Unlicense: their own licenses). None of it is in prebuilt/. A binary that statically links that
  runtime has obligations, which are "to verify" before any distribution (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md, COMPLIANCE.md).
- QuartzCore's default.metallib for the window server's Metal (tools/ca-metallib.py): Apple's; what you supply from your own Mac or IPSW,
  on your machine, never committed.
